==============================================================================
s02  Recurrence: sharing weights across time
      one matrix reused at every step, and what that does to the gradient
==============================================================================

2.1  The obvious fix does not work
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Part 1's first weight matrix has shape (8, 4): eight hidden units reading
  four features. The 4 is not a choice, it is the width of an IRIS row.

  The obvious repair is to give every position in the sequence its own
  weights. Two things go wrong, and the second is worse.
  sequence length T  parameters, per-position weights  parameters, shared  ratio
  -----------------  --------------------------------  ------------------  -----
                  5                               300                  60     5x
                 20                             1,200                  60    20x
                100                             6,000                  60   100x
               1000                            60,000                  60  1000x
  [PASS] per-position weights make the model's size depend on the longest sequence it may ever see
         60,000 parameters at T=1,000 against 60 shared, and a sequence one token longer than anything seen in training has no weights at all
  # the second problem has no table. With separate weights per position, whatever the network learns about a word at position 3 teaches it nothing about the same word at position 40 -- they are handled by different parameters that never see each other's gradients. Sharing the weights is not a memory optimisation. It is the statement that the rule for reading the next element does not depend on where you are in the sequence.

2.2  The recurrent network
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  s_t = tanh(W s_{t-1} + U x_t + b)
       the hidden state: the network's entire memory of everything before t
  eq:  y_t = V s_t
       the output, read off the state

  W, U and V are the same at every step. The state s_t is a fixed-width
  vector no matter how long the sequence is -- which is the whole trick,
  and later the whole problem.
  hidden width d                     6
  parameters                         84
  sequence length  states computed  final output width
  ---------------  ---------------  ------------------
                3                3                   3
               10               10                   3
               50               50                   3
              200              200                   3
  [PASS] the same parameters process any sequence length
         84 parameters handled lengths 3, 10, 50, 200 unchanged

2.3  Backpropagation through time
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Unroll the T steps and it is a T-layer network -- so Part 1's backward
  pass applies with no change. The one new feature is that every layer
  shares the same W, so the gradient does not arrive from one place.
  eq:  dJ_t/dW = sum_{k=0..t} (dJ_t/dy_t)(dy_t/ds_t)(ds_t/ds_k)(ds_k/dW)
       a sum over every step at which W acted
  eq:  delta_{k-1} = W^T diag(1 - s_{k-1}^2) delta_k
       Part 1's recursion; 1 - s^2 is tanh'(z) written through the activation
  eq:  dJ/dW = sum_k delta_k s_{k-1}^T
       accumulate, do not overwrite
  relative L2 error vs finite differences 4.810e-10
  [PASS] the derived backward pass through time is correct
         analytic gradient agrees with central finite differences to 4.8e-10 over all 36 recurrent weights, on a 12-step sequence
  [PASS] the accumulated gradient equals the sum of its per-step contributions
         sum over k of delta_k s_{k-1}^T reproduces dJ/dW exactly, which is the slide's sum made literal

2.4  Which steps actually supply the gradient
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The loss is scored on the final output only. So every one of the 12 terms
  in that sum is a message from a different point in the past, saying how
  much W's action *there* mattered to the answer *here*.
  step k  distance back from the loss  ||contribution||  share of total
  ------  ---------------------------  ----------------  --------------
       1                           11                 0           0.00%
       2                           10         0.0791139           0.56%
       3                            9          0.123779           0.87%
       6                            6          0.323733           2.28%
       9                            3           1.50973          10.66%
      11                            1           2.78098          19.63%
      12                            0           5.29682          37.38%
  # k = 1 is exactly zero for a structural reason rather than a numerical one: the contribution at step k is delta_k s_{k-1}^T, and s_0 is the zero vector, so W had nothing to act on at the first step. Every other row is the gradient genuinely arriving from that distance.
  [PASS] the gradient is dominated by the steps nearest the loss
         the last three steps contribute 72.0% of the total magnitude; the first three contribute 1.43%
  # this is the failure in miniature, and everything after it in this article is a response. The network can only learn to use information from step 1 if the gradient reaching step 1 is large enough to move the weights. Here it is roughly 2e-02 of the signal arriving from the recent steps. S3 shows this is not a quirk of this example -- it is geometric, and one number about W decides the rate.

2.5  The cost that has nothing to do with gradients
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  s_t cannot be computed until s_{t-1} exists. Every step waits for the one
  before it, however many processors are available.
  sequence length T  sequential steps required
  -----------------  -------------------------
                128                        128
               1024                       1024
               8192                       8192
  [PASS] recurrence forces a number of sequential steps equal to the sequence length
         s_t depends on s_{t-1} by construction, so no amount of hardware shortens the chain
  # worth parking rather than solving now. Even a recurrent network that learned perfectly would still read a 8,192-token document in 8,192 dependent steps. When S7 removes recurrence entirely, this is half of what it buys -- and the other half is the gradient problem S3 is about to make precise.

